  "findings": [
    "All 18 retained candidate PNGs are byte-identical to the current C163 profiled baseline. The eight differences from the older SIMD epoch are the impact/reaction frames affected by the intervening effect-anchor change, not this candidate.",
    "Candidate completed 787 live frames with zero refusals, CPU p95 8.3 ms and frame interval p95 16.8 ms. Four CPU frames exceed 1000/60 ms; strict performance remains RED.",
    "The new observed worst outlier is 55.1 ms at global 4266.4/local 969.733 ms, turn 1 approach. Nearest baseline time is only 0.1 ms later and costs 14.3 ms; candidate next-frame interval is 50 ms. Unprofiled evidence cannot attribute this to JIT, GC, scheduling or a particular algorithmic phase, and cannot justify dismissing it.",
    "Other slow phases align with existing hotspots: turn 1 ready 20.4 versus 24.3 ms; approach 16.9 versus 25.5 ms; approach 18.3 versus 19.9 ms. These are approximate-time comparisons, not identical sampled targets.",
    "The turn boundary already calls stage.play(), whose final operation is tick(), then stageAt() calls tick() again. Effect-player setup also occurs there. This establishes an existing doubled-work path at ready, without assigning the whole 20.4 ms to it.",
    "ARAP scratch and admission are constructed during creature-rig loading before READY. The new extra subset admission check is not constructed at the 4266.4 ms approach frame. Runtime compilation/tiering is not measured here.",
